# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("When a fabric layer under old pet turf blocks a clean",
         "<p>An older {city} pet install sometimes has a weed-barrier fabric sitting between the base and the turf, a layer newer pet builds skip on purpose because it holds liquid instead of letting it pass through. No amount of rinsing, power brooming, or enzyme treatment from above reaches urine trapped against a buried fabric layer, since the odor source sits below the infill a cleaning visit can actually work on. When a professional clean stops making a dent in a persistent smell on a lawn like that, the fabric underneath, not the cleaning method, is usually the real limit.</p>"),
        ("How compacted infill slows a base that used to drain fine",
         "<p>Years of dust, pollen, and foot traffic can pack infill down into something closer to a solid crust than the loose material it started as, and on a {city} lawn that crust slows how fast water actually reaches the washed rock base underneath even though the backing itself is still rated the same as the day it was installed. A rinse or a light broom moves surface debris but doesn't fully break up infill that's compacted this far, which is why a lawn that used to drain visibly fast can start holding water briefly at the surface without the base itself having failed.</p>"),
        ("The point where odor has moved past what cleaning reaches",
         "<p>An enzyme treatment worked into the infill handles odor that's sitting in the infill layer itself, but urine that's worked all the way through to the backing or the rock base below it on a {city} lawn is past the point a surface-level cleaning visit can fully resolve. A smell that returns within days of a proper deep clean, rather than weeks or months, is usually the sign that odor has migrated that far down, which points toward pulling and replacing the affected section rather than scheduling another cleaning visit on the same schedule.</p>"),
        ("An unwashed base holds moisture no cleaning schedule fixes",
         "<p>Turf installed on subgrade material that was never washed before it went down can develop a fines crust that traps moisture at the surface regardless of how often the turf above it gets cleaned. On a {city} lawn built this way, a spot that stays slightly damp longer than the rest of the yard after every cleaning visit is worth checking against the base itself rather than assuming a stronger cleaner or more frequent rinsing will eventually catch up. Cleaning treats what's happening at and above the infill, not a crusted layer sitting below it.</p>"),
        ("A high water table working against a cleaning schedule",
         "<p>Summer pushes the water table close to the surface on many {city} lots, and a base sitting at or near that saturation line can hold moisture from below at the same time a cleaning visit is trying to flush odor and debris down through it from above. A lawn on lower ground may need cleaning on a tighter schedule during the wettest months for exactly this reason, not because the cleaning method is doing anything wrong, but because the base underneath is fighting the process from the opposite direction during that stretch of the year.</p>"),
        ("Why the strip nearest a pond stays damp longer between visits",
         "<p>The section of a {city} lawn that sits within the setback strip near a pond or canal is often at the lowest part of the yard's overall grade, which means it holds moisture longer after a cleaning visit's rinse than turf on higher ground nearby does. Expecting that specific strip to dry on the same timeline as the rest of the lawn can make a cleaning schedule seem inconsistent when it's really the grade, not the cleaning method, driving the difference. Scheduling that section's follow-up check a little later than the rest of the yard accounts for the lag honestly.</p>"),
        ("Ground near a septic drainfield holds moisture differently",
         "<p>Soil near a septic drainfield on a {city} property can retain more moisture at the base level than the rest of a yard, simply because that ground processes wastewater as part of its normal function, and a cleaning visit that rinses that section as generously as anywhere else can leave it slower to fully drain afterward. This isn't a sign the cleaning was done incorrectly, just a reflection of what that specific patch of ground is already handling underneath the turf, and it's worth mentioning to a homeowner who might otherwise assume a cleaning problem.</p>"),
        ("Why a shock pad zone holds rinse water longer than the lawn beside it",
         "<p>The shock pad under a swing set or climbing structure on a {city} lawn drains differently than the standard washed-rock base just past its edge, and a cleaning visit's rinse water sometimes takes a little longer to clear that padded zone than the open turf right next to it. That's a function of the pad material itself, not a sign the cleaning missed anything, and knowing which sections of a property include a pad ahead of time helps set a realistic expectation for how quickly each part of the yard should look and feel dry again.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("What an enzyme cleaner is actually doing",
         "<p>An enzyme cleaner breaks down the specific organic compounds in urine and other residue that cause odor, rather than simply masking a smell with fragrance the way a basic deodorizer spray does on top of the blades. Worked into the infill on a {city} lawn and given time to sit before a final rinse, the enzymes continue acting on residue that's already below the surface, which is why a proper enzyme treatment holds up longer between visits than a product applied only to the visible pile.</p>"),
        ("Why zeolite needs a recharge, not just a top-up",
         "<p>Zeolite binds ammonia and odor compounds as liquid passes through it, but that binding capacity is finite, and once a given volume of zeolite on a {city} pet run has absorbed close to its limit, it stops doing much odor-control work even though it's still physically present in the yard. A recharge treatment, rather than simply adding fresh zeolite on top of saturated material, restores the infill's binding capacity across the whole layer. Waiting until a smell has clearly returned, rather than scheduling a recharge on a fixed interval, means living with the odor for longer than necessary.</p>"),
        ("Different infill, different cleaning approach",
         "<p>Plain silica sand rinses clean with a straightforward hose pass, but zeolite and coated sand need a gentler approach, since a heavy-pressure rinse can strip zeolite's active surface area or wear at a coated sand's protective layer faster than normal use would. On a {city} yard with mixed infill, cleaning a pet run built with zeolite calls for different handling than cleaning a family lawn section finished in silica sand, even though both sit under the same roofline and get serviced on the same visit.</p>"),
        ("Choosing a cleaner that won't strip a coated sand's coating",
         "<p>A cleaning product that's too harsh can wear away the non-toxic coating on coated silica sand faster than ordinary use would, which defeats the purpose of choosing that infill in the first place on a {city} pet system. A milder, pH-balanced cleaner paired with a proper rinse protects that coating while still doing the odor-control work an enzyme or antimicrobial product is meant to do. Matching the cleaning product to the infill actually installed matters more than defaulting to whatever cleaner happens to be strongest.</p>"),
        ("When a coated sand's protection has worn thin",
         "<p>Coated sand's antimicrobial protection wears down gradually over years of cleaning cycles and normal wear, and a {city} lawn that's had the same infill in place for several years may reach a point where topping it up no longer restores the odor control it once provided. At that stage, a partial or full infill exchange does more than another round of surface cleaning, since the coating itself, not just the volume of sand present, is what was doing the work originally.</p>"),
        ("What an enzyme product doesn't fix, even when it works well",
         "<p>An enzyme treatment breaks down odor-causing residue in the infill, but it doesn't restore a faded color, fix a matted or crushed pile, or rebuild a base that's compacted or crusted underneath a {city} lawn, jobs that belong to a different kind of visit entirely. Expecting an odor treatment to also solve a texture or drainage complaint sets up a cleaning visit to look like it failed when the product actually did exactly what it's designed to do. Separating what a cleaning product handles from what a repair or replacement addresses avoids that kind of mismatched expectation from the start.</p>"),
        ("A milder rinse for pollen and lovebug season, not a harsher one",
         "<p>Pollen dust and the sticky residue lovebugs leave behind on a {city} lawn during their respective seasons often need more than a plain hose pass to fully clear, but they don't call for a stronger chemical treatment either, since a mild surfactant rinse breaks down both kinds of residue without the risk a harsher degreaser carries for coated infill. Matching the product to the specific seasonal debris, rather than defaulting to either plain water or the strongest cleaner on hand, clears the residue without wearing down infill coatings faster than normal cleaning would.</p>"),
        ("Treating a shock pad zone at a different strength than the open lawn",
         "<p>The infill sitting over a {city} shock pad zone tends to hold odor and debris differently than the open lawn's standard base, often denser and slower to flush clean, which is part of why a cleaning treatment applied there sometimes needs a longer dwell time or a slightly different product strength than the rest of the property gets on the same visit. Treating the whole yard uniformly is simpler, but a pad zone that never quite matches the rest of the lawn's cleanliness after a standard pass may just need its own calibrated approach.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Locating the hot spots before the hose comes out",
         "<p>Before any water touches a {city} lawn, we walk it to locate the specific spots holding the most odor or the heaviest matting, usually along a fence line, near a gate, or wherever a dog's daily route concentrates traffic. That short assessment shapes where the enzyme treatment and power brooming get concentrated during the visit, rather than treating the whole yard uniformly when the actual problem is confined to a fraction of its square footage. Skipping this step means spreading effort evenly across a lawn where the need clearly isn't.</p>"),
        ("Clearing debris dry, before it gets pressed into the infill",
         "<p>Leaves, acorns, and windblown residue get raked or blown off a {city} lawn while everything is still dry, since wetting debris first presses it down into the infill layer instead of lifting it clear. A blower works faster across an open lawn, while a stiff rake handles debris caught against an edge or a fence line that a blower tends to push around rather than remove. Only once the surface is clear of loose material does the visit move on to any water-based step.</p>"),
        ("Giving an enzyme treatment time to work before rinsing",
         "<p>An enzyme or antimicrobial product needs contact time against the infill and backing to break down odor compounds, so on a {city} visit we let it sit for a set period rather than rinsing it away the moment it's applied. Rinsing too soon washes the active product out before it's finished working, leaving a lawn that smells fine for a day or two before the underlying odor returns. That dwell time is part of why a proper treatment visit takes longer than a quick rinse-and-broom appointment.</p>"),
        ("Checking infill depth against the original spec, not by eye",
         "<p>Rather than judging infill level by how the pile looks standing up, we check depth against the roughly one to two pounds per square foot a {city} lawn was originally built with, since infill can look adequate from a standing view while actually running thin closer to the backing. Topping up to that original spec, rather than adding infill until the surface looks right, keeps the drainage and odor-control performance consistent with how the system was designed rather than guessed at during a single visit.</p>"),
        ("A separate walk-through for anything that needs a repair call",
         "<p>Cleaning and repair are different jobs, so on a {city} visit we note anything found along the way, a loose edge, a thin patch, a seam starting to gap, as a separate item rather than attempting to fix it mid-clean. That walk-through happens at the end, once the lawn is rinsed and the infill's topped up, so any base or seam issue is visible against a clean surface instead of hidden under matted pile or debris that hasn't been cleared yet.</p>"),
        ("Adding a pass specifically for pollen and lovebug season",
         "<p>A {city} lawn's regular cleaning schedule sometimes needs an extra pass worked in during pollen season in spring and lovebug season in late summer, since both leave a residue on top of the pile that a routine monthly visit timed around a fixed calendar might miss entirely. Scheduling that additional visit around when those specific seasons actually peak, rather than waiting for the next scheduled date, keeps residue from sitting on the blades long enough to work down into the infill, where a simple rinse pass no longer clears it as easily.</p>"),
        ("Favoring plain water over a stronger product right at the water's edge",
         "<p>For the strip of {city} turf closest to a pond or canal, within the same setback zone the state's placement rule covers, a plain water rinse is often favored over an enzyme or antimicrobial product as a simple extra precaution, even though the placement rule itself governs turf material rather than cleaning chemicals. It's a judgment call rather than a requirement, but keeping stronger products confined to sections farther from open water is a reasonable habit for a crew working that close to a waterbody on a routine visit.</p>"),
        ("Scheduling a cleaning visit around a septic pump-out already booked",
         "<p>When a {city} homeowner already has a septic pump-out scheduled for the same week as a turf cleaning visit, sequencing the two so the ground near the drainfield isn't worked twice in a few days keeps a service crew's equipment and a cleaning crew's rinse routine from disturbing the same section back to back. Checking for that kind of overlap before confirming a cleaning appointment is a small coordination step that avoids compacting or oversaturating ground that's already being accessed for an unrelated reason that same week.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("A pressure washer at close range does more harm than good",
         "<p>A pressure washer held close to the turf on a {city} lawn can blow infill straight out of the backing, fray blade tips at the exact spot the nozzle passes over, and on an older lawn work a marginal seam loose that a gentler hose rinse would have left untouched. The narrow, concentrated stream that makes a pressure washer effective on concrete works against turf specifically, driving grit deeper into the backing's stitching rather than lifting it clear the way a wider hose spray does.</p>"),
        ("Why bleach works against what an enzyme cleaner is doing",
         "<p>Bleach can strip the coating off coated sand and, on a {city} pet run, it also breaks down the same enzymes a proper odor treatment relies on, so combining the two, or using bleach on a section already treated with an enzyme product, cancels out work that's already been done. Bleach residue left in the infill can also irritate a dog's paws on a run built specifically to be safe for daily pet use, which defeats the purpose of the infill chosen for that yard in the first place.</p>"),
        ("Metal rakes tear at blades a plastic tool wouldn't",
         "<p>A metal rake's tines can snag individual blades and pull them loose from the backing on a {city} lawn, especially along a seam where the stitching is already under some tension from normal use. A plastic rake or a stiff-bristle push broom clears the same leaves and debris without catching on the pile the way metal tines do. This matters most during heavy debris season, when the temptation to reach for whatever rake is closest is highest and the risk of snagged blades along an edge is easiest to overlook.</p>"),
        ("Letting oak leaves rot into the infill instead of clearing them",
         "<p>Live oaks drop their old leaves in late winter as new growth comes in, and a pile of wet leaves left sitting on a {city} lawn through a few afternoon storms starts to break down, releasing tannins that stain turf fiber and mixing organic matter directly into the infill layer. Once that debris has worked its way in rather than sitting on top, a simple rinse and broom no longer clears it, and the section affected usually needs the deeper power-brooming pass a routine visit would have prevented if the leaves had been cleared within a day or two.</p>"),
        ("Mixing cleaning chemicals that shouldn't go together",
         "<p>Combining an off-the-shelf deodorizer with a professional enzyme treatment, or layering a household cleaner over infill that was just recharged with zeolite, on a {city} lawn can neutralize whichever product went down second before it has a chance to work. Each product in a proper cleaning routine is chosen to interact with the infill and backing in a specific way, and stacking unrelated chemicals on top of each other between professional visits is more likely to cancel out an earlier treatment than to add any real benefit.</p>"),
        ("A cleaning product too strong for the pad it's applied to",
         "<p>Using the same strength product across a whole {city} lawn without accounting for a shock pad zone risks degrading the pad material faster than the surrounding turf's infill would show wear, since a pad isn't built to handle repeated exposure to a stronger treatment the way loose infill grains generally can. A pad that softens or breaks down early from cleaning chemicals rather than from normal use is a preventable problem, and it usually traces back to a crew treating a padded fall zone exactly like the rest of the yard instead of checking first.</p>"),
        ("Letting a septic lid disappear a little more with every visit",
         "<p>A cleaning crew focused on brushing and rinsing a {city} lawn's turf can walk right past a septic lid that's gradually being covered by infill drift or grass clippings with each visit, especially if nobody's specifically checking that spot. Left unaddressed over several cleaning cycles, a lid that was easy to find a year ago can become genuinely hard to locate, turning a routine pump-out call into an extra search. Flagging that spot during a regular visit costs nothing and keeps it from disappearing gradually.</p>"),
        ("A harsh degreaser on lovebug residue does what bleach does to coated sand",
         "<p>Reaching for a strong degreaser to cut through sticky lovebug residue on a {city} lawn can strip the protective coating off coated silica sand the same way bleach does, undoing the infill's antimicrobial protection well before it would have worn down on its own. The residue itself usually responds to a milder, purpose-matched product without needing that level of strength, so the stronger option isn't just unnecessary, it actively works against infill that was chosen specifically for its coating in the first place.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("A five-minute look-and-rinse habit between visits",
         "<p>A short weekly pass with the hose, walking the {city} yard while the water runs rather than just aiming it from one spot, catches early staining or a matted patch long before it needs a professional visit to fix. This isn't a deep clean, just enough attention to notice a change from the week before, which is usually the fastest way to keep a small problem from becoming the reason a scheduled cleaning visit runs longer than it should.</p>"),
        ("Treating a fresh accident before it has time to dry",
         "<p>A pet accident rinsed within the hour on a {city} lawn is far easier to clear than the same spot left to dry into the infill overnight, since fresh liquid is still sitting near the surface where a hose can flush it down through the base before bacteria have much time to work on it. Waiting for the next scheduled deep clean to address a fresh spot lets an easy fix turn into the kind of set-in odor that needs an enzyme treatment instead of a simple rinse.</p>"),
        ("Shortening the rinse interval through the hottest weeks",
         "<p>Heat speeds up the bacteria that turn urine into odor, so a rinse schedule that works fine on a {city} lawn in cooler months usually needs to tighten up through the hottest stretch of summer, when a gap of several days between rinses is more likely to let odor set in than the same gap would in spring or fall. Adjusting the interval seasonally, rather than sticking to one fixed schedule year-round, keeps the routine matched to what the weather is actually doing to the yard.</p>"),
        ("Keeping furniture off a spot that was just cleaned",
         "<p>Pile that's freshly brushed upright on a {city} lawn takes a little time to fully spring back to its resting position, and a planter or a piece of outdoor furniture set right back on top of a just-cleaned section can flatten it again before it's had the chance. Rotating furniture to a different spot for a few days after a cleaning visit, rather than putting everything back immediately, protects the work a power-brooming pass just did.</p>"),
        ("A simple check for whether infill needs a top-up",
         "<p>Pressing a finger into the pile in a high-traffic spot on a {city} lawn and seeing how much backing shows through is a rough but useful check a homeowner can do between visits, without needing a scale or a depth gauge. A traffic lane where the backing is visibly closer to the surface than it is in a shaded corner of the same yard usually means that section needs infill sooner than the rest, which is worth mentioning when a cleaning visit gets scheduled.</p>"),
        ("A closer spring look at the strip nearest the water",
         "<p>The section of a {city} lawn within the setback strip near a pond or canal is worth a slightly closer look each spring than the rest of the yard, since that low, damp ground tends to hold organic buildup and humidity a little longer after cleaning than turf on higher ground does. A homeowner who checks that specific strip separately, rather than assuming the whole lawn ages the same way, catches a slower-draining or slightly odorous patch before it becomes noticeable from the rest of the yard.</p>"),
        ("Timing an extra rinse to when pollen and lovebugs actually peak",
         "<p>Rather than waiting for the next scheduled cleaning visit, a {city} homeowner can add a quick rinse timed specifically to when pollen counts or lovebug activity actually peak in the yard, since both leave a residue that sits on top of the pile rather than settling in gradually the way ordinary dust does. A five-minute hose pass during that window clears most of it before it has a chance to work down into the infill, where it would otherwise need a more thorough treatment to fully remove later.</p>"),
        ("A second-year check on whether the cleaning interval still fits",
         "<p>Once a {city} lawn has been through a full second year, it's worth comparing how well a section still smells and drains against how it performed after the first year, since infill and backing both change gradually with age in ways that can call for a tighter cleaning interval than the schedule set at installation. A lawn that used to go a month between rinses without any odor building up may need that interval shortened as it ages, and a second-year comparison is a natural point to make that call.</p>"),
    ],
}
